{# The shell grammar (docs/DESIGN.md § Site shell, issue 192) as macros.

   These render the cross-page chrome every page shares: the nav band, the page
   head, and the footer band with its provenance line. They were Python string
   builders in `rendering/shell.py` until issue 241; moving them here is what
   makes autoescaping the default for the shell rather than a per-call
   `html.escape` discipline (docs/FRONTEND.md § Server-side templates).

   Each macro emits its fragment with no internal newlines, exactly as the
   builders it replaced did. That is not incidental formatting: the band and
   footer are flex rows of inline-level children, so whitespace between them is
   rendered whitespace, and the committed markup-parity fixtures in
   `tests/js/fixtures/` are compared byte for byte.

   `site_name`, `contact_href`, `opens_in_new_tab`, `external_link_attributes`,
   and the icon builders are environment globals, registered in
   `rendering/documents.py`. Macros do not see their caller's context, and the
   shell is rendered from inside macros almost everywhere, so passing these per
   render would silently cover only the handful of uses outside a macro. #}

{% macro head_links(origin, shareable=true, image_path='/og-image.png') -%}
{# The brand-asset head block shared by every page: icons and og:image.

   `origin` is the canonical https origin used for the absolute og:image URL;
   the icon links stay root-relative so any origin serves its own copies.
   `shareable` controls the accompanying twitter:card tag: every page meant to
   be shared pairs og:image with summary_large_image (the default); the 404
   passes shareable=false to keep the wide image without implying the page
   itself is a shareable card.

   `image_path` is the root-relative card this page unfurls with. It defaults to
   the site-wide brand card, which is what every page shared before issue #136
   and what every page but one still shares: a race page passes its own
   build-time card instead, because a link to a race that unfurled as the brand
   said nothing about the race (`rendering/og_image.py`). It is a path rather
   than a URL for the same reason the icons are — the origin is supplied here,
   once — and it is made absolute below because crawlers require that of
   `og:image` specifically.

   The two-space continuation indent is the head's, because this block is
   emitted inside `<head>` where its first line is already indented. The indent
   that follows the twitter:card line sits inside the `if`, and `{%+` keeps
   `lstrip_blocks` from eating it, so an unshareable page starts flush with the
   `og:image` tag exactly as the builder this replaced did. -#}
{% if shareable %}<meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image">
  {%+ endif %}<meta property="og:image" content="{{ origin }}{{ image_path }}">
  <link rel="icon" href="/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml">
  <link rel="icon" href="/favicon-32.png" type="image/png" sizes="32x32">
  <link rel="apple-touch-icon" href="/apple-touch-icon.png">
{%- endmacro %}


{% macro social_meta(document_title, description, canonical_url) -%}
{#- The social-card and canonical grammar every full-content page renders.

   The companion to `head_links`, which carries the half of the same policy
   that is the same on every page (the card type and image). This half varies
   only in its three values, so it is one macro rather than eight lines copied
   into each template — a change to how the site describes itself to a crawler
   or a share sheet is one edit, not five.

   The 404 deliberately does not call this: it is `noindex` with no canonical
   and no description, and carries only the two title tags, so routing it
   through here would mean conditionals for a page that wants none of it.

   Indented like `head_links` and for the same reason — the call site supplies
   the first line's two spaces, and the continuations carry their own. -#}
<meta name="description" content="{{ description }}">
  <meta property="og:type" content="website">
  <meta property="og:title" content="{{ document_title }}">
  <meta property="og:description" content="{{ description }}">
  <meta property="og:url" content="{{ canonical_url }}">
  <meta name="twitter:title" content="{{ document_title }}">
  <meta name="twitter:description" content="{{ description }}">
  <link rel="canonical" href="{{ canonical_url }}">
{%- endmacro %}


{% macro nav_link(label, href, key, current, sources_link=false) -%}
<a href="{{ href }}"{% if sources_link %} data-sources-link{% endif %}{% if current == key %} aria-current="page"{% endif %}>{{ label }}</a>
{%- endmacro %}


{% macro band(guide_href, sources_href, compare_href=none, corrections_href=none, about_href='/about/', current=none, sources_link_data_attribute=false, shareable=true) -%}
{# Slot 1 of the shell grammar: brand, nav, and page actions.

   `current` names this page's nav entry (`endorsements`, `comparisons`,
   `corrections`, `sources`, or `about`). The brand lockup links to
   `guide_href` — the current
   election's endorsements page — rather than `/`, which only redirects there;
   that link target is also what R3 keys the extended masthead off.

   The nav is one `<details>` disclosure at every width. Above the shell
   breakpoint CSS forces its panel visible and hides the summary, so it renders
   as the familiar inline row; below it, the summary becomes a control reading
   "Pages" and the panel drops beneath. This needs no JavaScript, which matters
   because the archived guides are frozen files.

   `shareable` follows the same flag that governs a page's social card (R2), so
   the 404 gets neither an og card nor a Share action from one property.
   `sources_link_data_attribute` adds the guide's `data-sources-link` hook so
   its entry module can carry the reader's live selection onto the Sources
   page; lit does not own shell markup, so that href is patched imperatively. #}
<div class="site-band"><a class="site-brand" href="{{ guide_href }}">{{ site_icon_svg(on_dark=true) }}<span>{{ site_name }}</span></a><div class="site-band-actions">
{%- if shareable -%}
<button type="button" class="band-icon-action" data-shell-share aria-label="Share this page" title="Share this page">{{ share_icon_svg() }}</button>
{%- endif -%}
<details class="site-band-menu"><summary>Pages</summary><nav aria-label="Site">
{#- Reading order follows dependency: the guide is the destination, Sources is
    what feeds it, Comparisons is a view derived from those sources,
    Corrections is the archive talking about itself after publication
    (docs/RESULTS.md, "The corrections page"; issue #290), and How this
    works explains all four. -#}
{{- nav_link('Endorsements', guide_href, 'endorsements', current) -}}
{{- nav_link('Sources', sources_href, 'sources', current, sources_link_data_attribute) -}}
{%- if compare_href is not none -%}
{{- nav_link('Comparisons', compare_href, 'comparisons', current) -}}
{%- endif -%}
{%- if corrections_href is not none -%}
{{- nav_link('Corrections', corrections_href, 'corrections', current) -}}
{%- endif -%}
{{- nav_link('How this works', about_href, 'about', current) -}}
</nav></details></div></div>
{%- if shareable -%}
<p class="visually-hidden" role="status" aria-live="polite" data-shell-share-status></p>
{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro %}


{% macro page_head(title, eyebrow=none, mode='plain') -%}
{# Slot 2 of the shell grammar: one page head for every page.

   `eyebrow` is the election name on election-scoped pages and `none` on
   agnostic ones, where its absence is the only signal needed (R2). `title` is
   the page's own name, agreeing with its nav label and `<title>` (R5), and for
   election-scoped pages it is a plural noun so eyebrow and title read as one
   name — "August 2026 Primary Comparisons" (R5a).

   **The tagline is the caller's `{% call %}` block, not a parameter.** It
   carries inline markup on purpose — entities in the guide's copy, real links
   on the 404 — so it is authored as template markup by the calling page,
   where a literal `&rsquo;` stays literal and an interpolated value is escaped
   like anything else. That is the same asymmetry the old `tagline_html`
   parameter had, made structural instead of advisory: escaping used to be the
   caller's to remember (`_about_html` passed `html.escape(...)`, and the
   `_html` suffix carried the warning), and a caller who forgot got an
   injection. Now a tagline that wants markup opts in by writing markup, and
   everything interpolated is escaped by default.

   `mode` selects the head's one legal variation, as a closed set rather than
   independent flags, so a shape the grammar does not define cannot be
   expressed:

   - `plain` — the head sits below the masthead on paper.
   - `extended` — the masthead's navy runs through the head instead of stopping
     at the band. This is the single exception the brand-link target buys
     (R3/R4), and it bends no other rule: the ground-relative color rules
     already carry the eyebrow to mint and the title to white.
   - `measured` — the head takes the same ~46rem reading column its page's body
     uses, reusing the shared `.narrow-main` class. DESIGN.md § Typography
     holds that the frame is the site and the measure is the content, so a head
     whose page sets a book measure must sit on it too, or its tagline outruns
     the prose directly beneath it. #}
{%- set variant = {'plain': '', 'extended': ' extended', 'measured': ' narrow'}[mode] -%}
<header class="page-head{{ variant }}">
{%- if mode == 'measured' %}<div class="narrow-main">{% endif -%}
{%- if eyebrow is not none %}<p class="page-eyebrow">{{ eyebrow }}</p>{% endif -%}
<h1>{{ title }}</h1><p class="page-tagline">{{ caller() }}</p>
{%- if mode == 'measured' %}</div>{% endif -%}
</header>
{%- endmacro %}


{% macro footer_audit(data_updated_date, site_updated_date, data_version, git_commit, project_url, data_href, source_panel_id=none, source_panel_hash=none) -%}
{# The shared two-line data/site provenance grammar. #}
<span class="audit-data">Data updated {{ data_updated_date }} (<a href="{{ data_href }}">{{ data_version[:12] }}</a>)
{%- if source_panel_id is not none and source_panel_hash is not none %} · Panel {{ source_panel_id }} ({{ source_panel_hash[:12] }}){% endif -%}
</span><span class="audit-join" aria-hidden="true"> · </span><span class="audit-site">Site updated {{ site_updated_date }} (<a href="{{ project_url }}/commit/{{ git_commit }}"{{ external_link_attributes }}>{{ git_commit[:12] }}</a>)</span>
{%- endmacro %}


{% macro footer_icon_link(label, href, svg, external=false) -%}
{# An icon-only control has no visible text to carry the new-tab hint, so it
   goes in the accessible name instead. #}
{%- set accessible_label = label ~ (opens_in_new_tab if external else '') -%}
<a class="footer-icon-action" href="{{ href }}" aria-label="{{ accessible_label }}" title="{{ accessible_label }}"{% if external %}{{ external_link_attributes }}{% endif %}>{{ svg }}</a>
{%- endmacro %}


{% macro footer_band(project_url, audit, about_href='/about/') -%}
{# The navy footer band, rendered by every page including the 404 (item L55).

   The builder this replaced was documented as "shared by every page except the
   404", which its own caller contradicted: `_not_found_html` rendered this band
   like everything else, and still does. Only the *audit* line varies — the
   site-wide pages carry no source panel — and only the Share action is
   genuinely absent from the 404, up in the masthead.

   Mirrors `band`: the same icon+wordmark lockup, linking home, on the left; a
   centered icon action cluster on the right — Contact, source/audit files on
   GitHub, and How this works.

   Share moved to the masthead in issue 192, under the rule promoted to
   DESIGN.md § Site shell: *masthead = actions on the page; footer = meta about
   the site.* Issue 193 retired the generated PDF edition, taking the Printable
   PDF action with it — the browser's own print output is the printable one
   now. #}
<div class="site-footer-band"><a class="site-footer-brand" href="/" aria-label="{{ site_name }}">{{ site_icon_svg(on_dark=true) }}<span>{{ site_name }}</span></a><div class="site-footer-audit">{{ audit }}</div><div class="site-footer-actions">
{{- footer_icon_link('Contact', contact_href, envelope_icon_svg()) -}}
{{- footer_icon_link('Source and audit files on GitHub', project_url, github_icon_svg(), external=true) -}}
{{- footer_icon_link('How this works', about_href, info_icon_svg()) -}}
</div></div>
{%- endmacro %}


{% macro election_footer_band(project_url, metadata, guide_path, data_updated_date, site_updated_date) -%}
{# Election-page provenance, composed once for the guide, Sources, and
   Comparisons. The site-wide pages (About, the archive) have no election
   metadata and no source panel, so they call `footer_band` with their own
   `footer_audit` directly. #}
{{- footer_band(project_url, footer_audit(data_updated_date, site_updated_date, metadata.data_version, metadata.git_commit, project_url, guide_path ~ 'release-manifest.json', metadata.source_panel_id, metadata.source_panel_hash)) -}}
{%- endmacro %}
